\ Container construction, mutation, and access produce the same values through
\ the direct-call codegen as the interpreter. Under runtime-image AOT the word
\ bodies are compiled, so the printed results come from the direct native calls
\ for `make-mutable-map`, `make-vector`, `@set!`, `@set`, and `@get`. The
\ values match the interpreter: a mutated mutable-map reads back its value, a
\ vector indexes its second element, and an immutable `@set` on a hash reads
\ back through `@get`.
M{ } "k" 1 @set! "k" @get .
V{ 10 20 30 } 1 #nth .
H{ } "key" 2 @set "key" @get .
